WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.268 line:0%
-Our next guest is a comedian

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:04.070 line:0%
you know from his work
in the "Jackass" franchise.

00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:05.805 line:0%
His book,
"A Hard Kick in the Nuts:

00:00:05.805 --> 00:00:07.841 line:0%
What I've Learned from a
Lifetime of Terrible Decisions,"

00:00:07.841 --> 00:00:11.077 line:0%
is out now. Please welcome
to the show Steve-O, everybody!

00:00:11.077 --> 00:00:12.812
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:12.812 --> 00:00:21.320 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:29.796 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:29.796 --> 00:00:32.097
Steve-O, I'm very happy
to have you here.

00:00:32.097 --> 00:00:35.634
And I don't think it will come
to anyone's surprise here

00:00:35.634 --> 00:00:38.437
that this was not
a staged photo.

00:00:38.437 --> 00:00:40.540
You really did allow yourself
to be kicked in the nuts

00:00:40.540 --> 00:00:41.974
for this picture.
-I insisted on it.

00:00:41.974 --> 00:00:43.742
-You insisted on it.
-I did.

00:00:43.742 --> 00:00:46.078
-It looks -- Do you have to get

00:00:46.078 --> 00:00:47.947
the right kind of person
to be the kicker?

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:51.451
Because I imagine
that's a big part of it.

00:00:51.451 --> 00:00:54.119
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.119 --> 00:00:57.123
-You want someone who's
going to be stern but fair.

00:00:57.123 --> 00:00:59.458
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:59.458 --> 00:01:02.228
Now, you've also given out
some kicks over the years.

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:03.863
-I have. I have.
-Yeah.

00:01:03.863 --> 00:01:09.735
-I'm absolutely confident
that I have been both asked

00:01:09.735 --> 00:01:12.304
to kick people in the nuts

00:01:12.304 --> 00:01:14.140
and have kicked people
in the nuts

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:15.541
more than anybody
who has ever lived.

00:01:15.541 --> 00:01:17.042
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:01:17.042 --> 00:01:19.712
-So if someone sees you out and
about, that might be the thing

00:01:19.712 --> 00:01:21.414
they want even more
than a selfie?

00:01:21.414 --> 00:01:23.916
-Oh. Oh. It's on a daily basis.
-Really?

00:01:23.916 --> 00:01:25.918
-And they'll be passionate
about it.

00:01:25.918 --> 00:01:28.988
Like, "Dude, is there any way

00:01:28.988 --> 00:01:31.490
I can get you
to kick me in the nuts?"

00:01:31.490 --> 00:01:35.895
And my position on it
used to be,

00:01:35.895 --> 00:01:37.663
"Who am I to dash
a man's dreams?"

00:01:37.663 --> 00:01:39.999
-Sure. Very kind of you.
You're very giving.

00:01:39.999 --> 00:01:43.236
-But I've since matured,

00:01:43.236 --> 00:01:45.738
and as such, I've retired
from nut kicking.

00:01:45.738 --> 00:01:49.241
-That's very both understandable
and a little --

00:01:49.241 --> 00:01:50.977
I don't know. A little sad.
You know?

00:01:50.977 --> 00:01:52.612
-It is. It is.

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.781
But that's just not
the kind of energy

00:01:54.781 --> 00:01:56.282
I'm trying to put out there
anymore.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:58.284
-Yes.
Well, I like that there's --

00:01:58.284 --> 00:02:01.354
This book is sort of a mix
of the old and the new.

00:02:01.354 --> 00:02:03.456
-Yeah.
-I think maybe an example

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:04.991
of the old is,
to promote the book,

00:02:04.991 --> 00:02:06.793
you went driving on a truck

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.261
in sort of I guess, like,
a porch swing? Is that...?

00:02:09.261 --> 00:02:11.730
-It was exactly a porch swing.
-Uh-huh.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:14.867
We built a porch swing
onto a cube truck.

00:02:14.867 --> 00:02:17.736
And that's me
going over 50 miles an hour

00:02:17.736 --> 00:02:20.339
down a freeway in Los Angeles.
-Gotcha.

00:02:20.339 --> 00:02:24.377
Now, I think people assume that
you are a man without fear,

00:02:24.377 --> 00:02:26.245
but that would actually
not be true.

00:02:26.245 --> 00:02:28.881
And I would imagine one thing
is that you have to face

00:02:28.881 --> 00:02:31.417
a lot of your fears when
you write a book like this.

00:02:31.417 --> 00:02:33.352
-Yeah.
-And this book actually starts

00:02:33.352 --> 00:02:35.054
by saying, "I started
this book as a joke,"

00:02:35.054 --> 00:02:36.555
but then it gets very honest

00:02:36.555 --> 00:02:38.925
about the issues
you've faced over your life,

00:02:38.925 --> 00:02:40.526
including issues like addiction.

00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:43.195
Did that -- Was that the plan
when you started?

00:02:43.195 --> 00:02:45.565
Or did you sort of find that
in the writing?

00:02:45.565 --> 00:02:49.201
-I put out a memoir in 2011.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:51.337
It was a "New York Times"
Best Seller.

00:02:51.337 --> 00:02:54.040
It has a five-star rating
on Amazon.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:57.176
Like, by every measure,
it was my proudest work.

00:02:57.176 --> 00:03:00.213
And over the course
of 10 years,

00:03:00.213 --> 00:03:02.882
since that book came out,
I thought,

00:03:02.882 --> 00:03:07.386
"Man, my life has been, like,
even crazier since then.

00:03:07.386 --> 00:03:09.055
I got another book in me."

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:12.124
So I reached out
to my literary agent, who said,

00:03:12.124 --> 00:03:16.229
"Sadly, "Memoir Part 2'
is not a thing.

00:03:16.229 --> 00:03:19.765
So you need to come up
with some kind of angle on it."

00:03:19.765 --> 00:03:24.036
And what I landed on
was a book of wisdom

00:03:24.036 --> 00:03:26.972
gleaned from a lifetime
of terrible decisions

00:03:26.972 --> 00:03:28.908
to serve as a vehicle

00:03:28.908 --> 00:03:31.210
for telling all the crazy
stories from the last 10 years.

00:03:31.210 --> 00:03:32.745
-Yeah.

00:03:32.745 --> 00:03:35.281
So it's basically a memoir
wrapped up in a self-help book.

00:03:35.281 --> 00:03:36.482
-Correct.

00:03:36.482 --> 00:03:37.984
-But it does actually
have elements of both.

00:03:37.984 --> 00:03:41.720
-Right. It's about 90%
"Wow, is this guy nuts?"

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.792
and 10% "He might have learned
something from that."

00:03:45.792 --> 00:03:47.960
-Another fear you faced is,

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:51.130
you know, sort of later in life,
you started doing standup,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:53.666
which I think might be scarier
to some people

00:03:53.666 --> 00:03:56.502
than any of the stunts
you've done in the films.

00:03:56.502 --> 00:03:58.637
Was it scary for you
the first time you did it?

00:03:58.637 --> 00:04:00.038
How did it come about?

00:04:00.038 --> 00:04:03.008
-Well, the first time I did it
was now 16 years ago.

00:04:03.008 --> 00:04:06.278
2006, I got invited
to a comedy club.

00:04:06.278 --> 00:04:10.316
Somebody asked me to do a stunt
on stage at the comedy club.

00:04:10.316 --> 00:04:14.020
I showed up totally wasted,
no plan,

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:16.756
walked in and thought,
"Wow, the craziest stunt

00:04:16.756 --> 00:04:19.859
I could possibly do
would be to try standup."

00:04:19.859 --> 00:04:23.329
And I was scared.

00:04:23.329 --> 00:04:26.732
I came up with one joke that
I'm not allowed to say on TV.

00:04:26.732 --> 00:04:28.768
-Okay.
-It got a laugh.

00:04:28.768 --> 00:04:30.536
And I was addicted
to the idea of it.

00:04:30.536 --> 00:04:32.805
Ended up really, really
going for it.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:36.141
And, yes, in the beginning,
it was more scary than anything.

00:04:36.141 --> 00:04:39.611
-One of the things --
As a huge fan of the franchise

00:04:39.611 --> 00:04:41.313
and of all of you guys --

00:04:41.313 --> 00:04:44.917
And I know you guys
have coped with success,

00:04:44.917 --> 00:04:46.986
which is often really tricky
for a group of friends.

00:04:46.986 --> 00:04:48.654
-Yeah.
-Had to cope with tragedy,

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:50.823
obviously, which is
even harder to deal with.

00:04:50.823 --> 00:04:52.558
But it's been really special

00:04:52.558 --> 00:04:56.295
to watch you guys
continue to make content.

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:57.530
-Right.

00:04:57.530 --> 00:04:59.731
-Do you feel that
when you're doing it?

00:04:59.731 --> 00:05:01.000
Like, "This is insane

00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:02.735
that 20 years into it,
we're still all together?"

00:05:02.735 --> 00:05:04.304
-Everything about it

00:05:04.304 --> 00:05:07.974
is absolutely shocking
and unbelievable.

00:05:07.974 --> 00:05:09.842
-Yeah.
-I mean, if --

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:14.079
Like, the fact that it even
started is pretty shocking,

00:05:14.079 --> 00:05:17.550
let alone that it's endured
for decades

00:05:17.550 --> 00:05:20.352
and the fact
that we're all still friends.

00:05:20.352 --> 00:05:23.789
You know?
We're basically a band.

00:05:23.789 --> 00:05:25.524
"Jackass" is like a rock band

00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:29.261
that just doesn't have any
talent for music or anything.

00:05:29.261 --> 00:05:31.364
-I've heard you call it
a "wholesome" show,

00:05:31.364 --> 00:05:33.098
which I don't think
that would be what

00:05:33.098 --> 00:05:34.600
a lot of people would say.

00:05:34.600 --> 00:05:37.437
Make a case real quick as to why
"Jackass" is wholesome.

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:39.972
-I absolutely maintain
that it's wholesome

00:05:39.972 --> 00:05:43.642
because it's devoid
of anything mean-spirited.

00:05:43.642 --> 00:05:45.677
There's nothing hateful
about it.

00:05:45.677 --> 00:05:49.248
Sure, we give ourselves
and each other a very hard time,

00:05:49.248 --> 00:05:51.750
but we're all such attention
whores, we're begging for it.

00:05:51.750 --> 00:05:53.419
-Yeah.
-So...

00:05:53.419 --> 00:05:55.221
It's completely permissible

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:57.557
that we are so hard on ourselves
and each other.

00:05:57.557 --> 00:06:00.125
And as far as anybody else goes,

00:06:00.125 --> 00:06:02.261
there's just nothing
mean-spirited about it.

00:06:02.261 --> 00:06:04.630
The spirit of "Jackass"
is genuinely wholesome.

00:06:04.630 --> 00:06:08.768
We're just here
to brighten up people's day.

00:06:08.768 --> 00:06:11.470
-Well, I want to thank you
because mine

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:13.772
has been brightened up many
times over the last two decades.

00:06:13.772 --> 00:06:15.575
-Well, thank you.
-Thank you for being here.

00:06:15.575 --> 00:06:17.276
Congrats on the book.
Come back soon, man.

00:06:17.276 --> 00:06:19.745
Really appreciate it.
Steve-O, everybody.

00:06:19.745 --> 00:06:22.047
"A Hard Kick in the Nuts"
is out now.

00:06:22.047 --> 00:06:25.051
We'll be right back with
Dick Ebersol, everyone.

